 successful you charge  more eventually now how did you segue I  first encountered your work in the 90s  always in a music contest yes Flyers  record covers uh how did you say way out  of that I don't think you're doing much  of that anymore if I'm correct and how  did you segue out of that and did you  always want to segue out of that well a  lot of it has to do with Robert Williams  and Robert Williams who is a huge  influence on me an amazing painter I  mean he he he was one of the co-creators  of the magazine juxtapose I mean he's  he's a big deal yeah  now that was a magazine that sort of  took the style of art you do which I  know people call lowbrow I don't know if  you embrace that term I'm sort of using  what's considered junky iconography in  American culture cars hot rods moms  robots and sort of melding it into a  gallery mentality is that yeah that's  good enough yeah and I mean Bob had  always sort of impressed upon me that I  needed to be doing work in a fine art  context as well as doing commercial work  and and I I had been for a very long  time and then about 2003 I just decided  like if I'm gonna do this I have to do  it like I can't continue to straddle  these two things I have to commit to  doing fine art full-time so I I ended up  at the time I worked out of my home I  ended up renting a studio at the brewery  in downtown LA and with the notion that  I was going to start painting these sort  of large-scale paintings and so the  first piece I did was a it was thirteen  six foot panels so it was 78 feet long I  had this goal that I wanted to do  something in the vein of names  Rosenquist like a big epic painting that  would just sort of you know throw the  gauntlet down and say okay this is what  I'm doing now I mean you're limiting  yourself with the Audion that's that's  big yes I I could not fit that in my the  idea was there were a few people at the  time that were sort of the whales of the  you know art Colette my art collecting  world and eye contact as I was working  on the painting I started contacting  them independently and saying you know  sending them photos and saying here look  at this you know I'm trying to finish  this big huge epic thing and if I'm  gonna finish it I need I need a patron I  need somebody to come in and step up and  say yeah I'm gonna buy this and  eventually that did happen and I had  someone purchase it or agree to purchase  it at about the halfway point of the  completion so at that point I was able  to just do the rest of the work and not  worry about what  what I was gonna do with it and that was  a really lucky thing for me and it sort  of saved saved my bacon you know because  I I mean I part of the reason I painted  it in segments was and James Rosenquist  did the same thing when he painted f-111  was he had the idea well if I can't sell  this as one piece I can take all the  chunks of it and sell it off to people  individually and that was sort of in the  back of my mind as well so this first  giant thing that went straight from your  studio to a private owner well to a  gallery no I did actually have a gallery  show but the work was already sold so  basically the gallery show is just a you  know a way to present it to the public  and let people see it and and the  collector who bought it is actually now  talking about building building his own  museum which would mean that it would be  on permanent display with a lot of other  people like Mark Ryden and Robert  Williams and other you know sort of big  wheels so is is the lobar art movement  very West Coast centric I mean as it's  broken through to to New York yet you  have any in din that world well it's I  mean Robert Williams has been showing  with tonisha Frazee back east at least  15 years now but I don't know it's funny  because I really feel like at this point  in New York City and the New York art  scene is kind of behind that's behind  the times like I think there's a lot of  amazing stuff happening on the 