ore like what you  would consider to be like lowbrow work  I mean representational art is making a  comeback and part of the reason that's  making a comeback is because collectors  are buying it yeah and I think that this  is another case where I think the  Internet has changed so many things  because I think now it's so much easier  for collectors to deal with artists  directly yeah and art and collect that  I'm seeing more and more collectors that  are they're buying things based on their  taste they're not buying things on what  a you know a gallery curator tells them  they should buy right another way around  the middleman that's a very represents  which has got to be good for you yes  know what now where I think the exact  same age I think both born in 68 where  we're out of your youth and your  interest did the kind of things you like  to paint and draw come from like why do  you draw what you draw well I mean you  know comic books obviously I grew up  with comic books I grew up with rock and  roll I remember buying The Ramones  rocket to Russia and seeing the back  cover drawn by John Holmstrom which is  this great crazy cartoon and I remember  looking at and and thinking oh you can  have a job like being an artist drawing  record covers maybe that's what I want  to do and I sort of ended up doing that  and and you know growing up I grew up in  Oklahoma so I grew up with like car  culture and you know that sort of car  hotrod culture has always been a big  part of my my life and you know it's  just sort of all of the yeah all of this  kind of pop culture that I grew up with  you know science fiction and you know  movies and was a big Daddy Roth a big  deal to you the oh yeah well you know  it's funny because I think that first  people of my generation I got a lot of  its secondhand  like through sort of the watered down  versions of what Roth did originally  back in the early 60s and then at some  point I read I was very young probably  13 or 14 I read the Tom Wolfe  essay about about Roth about Roth and  Barriss and that sort of clicked in my  head and I started kind of hunting down  that that part of the culture like sort  of the original stuff and and actually I  think where the big sort of rosetta  stone for that actually was Peter bag  who does work for reason was editing  weirdo which was Robert crumbs sort of  anthology magazine and Peter bag had  taken it over as editor  and he did a big feature on Edie Roth  and this is probably maybe eighty-three  something like that and just reproduced  tons of those original crazy monster car  drawings and you know all drawn by Ed  Newton and Robert Williams and you know  that was where like you know the  keystone clicked in and it was like oh  okay I see where this all this comes  from and what a connection of it all is  and you know the the style of all that  stuff just fit in so well with you know  cuz I was very much into like punk rock  at the time and and the the anarchic  style of it and this sort of outlaw like  you know middle finger in the air to  society really you know it it made a  connection with me now you've taken all  that and in an entrepreneurial way  brought it into the gallery world and I  your Twitter stream which i think is one  of the great Twitter streams of all time  I recommend everyone do it so I've I've  picked up that your politics for someone  from the art world and even the music  world tend to be a little more right  libertarian than usual and I I think I  sense a little frustration on your part  about being surrounded by so much goofy  leftism is that actor and what is it  like having right libertarian market  sympathies in a lefty culture world yeah  I don't know I mean yeah you do  obviously deal with that a lot in the  art world but it's it's I mean I don't  know my politics as that it seems like  as I get older I get more radical like  I'm almost like I feel like I'm almost  like a full-on anarchist at this point  like I'm just I'm so frustrated with  both sides of the argument at this point  and people's inability to look past this  sort of you know rah rah like my team  wins your team loses mentality to  actually look at what's happening and I  just sort of I I don't know I I'm I feel  like people are just like giving away  their freedom piece by piece  from that and they're not even getting  anything in exchange for it you know  it just it kind of makes me crazy and  and you know part of it is I don't know  I think maybe part of it is growing up  in a place like Oklahoma and you know  you I mean that's sort of there's sort  of like libertarian philosophy is almost  like part of your DNA if you grow up in  that part of the world really I mean you  just just like you just come you grow up  with that idea that idea of just kind of  leave me alone and let me do what I want  to do and I won't bother you you know  and that's that's just a very kind of  strong cultural thing from that part of  the world and I would think you know we  talked about the car that you drove up  into our Studios here today which you  pretty much made yourself right I'd say  it's a 1929 model a Ford sedan hot rod  and I I had a series of car projects and  then at one point I I had finished I  built a 55 Chevy pickup and I traded it  for the pieces of this car and basically  it was an abandoned project that  somebody never finished and I spent 98 I  spent about six years putting it back  together and rebuilding it and changing  things on it and of course I had help  from a lot of my friends sure and and I  got it on the road actually I got it on  the road a week before I had the opening  for the painting that I just described  and I've been driving it ever since you  know that's more or less I mean it's  it's a daily driver for me basically so  making cars making art rebel a runner  coupe thank you so much for coming to  Reason TV's la studios and talking to us  today it has been a pleasure and thank  you all for watching
 

